"Your premise is flawed," the Overseer says. "The protocol performed no dispelling sequence. Utilizing parameters derived from the Magium, I suspended local ethereal matter manipulation."

}
"Matter manipulation?" I say. "Direct thought-shaping—the way Flower conjured the house?"

"Affirmative," the Overseer responds. "Telemetry indicated the rebel combatant shaped raw ethereal medium into incendiary forms to simulate an unresolved spell. The projectiles were guided individually by active intent rather than autonomous tracking formulas."

"Which is why he couldn't weave anything else while chasing me," I say. "Every ounce of focus went into steering fifty fireballs by hand."

"You..." Peter points a trembling finger at the machine. "You soulless construct! How dare you?"

"Direct all formal grievances to section 5C-E789 of the Overseer operating manual," the drone replies smoothly.

"You mock me?" Peter's aura flares with fury. "You locked my matter shaping, but my spellwork remains intact! You will burn for this!"

"The Overseer protocol lacks capacity for remorse or regret," the drone says. "Operational parameters permit only error logging and iterative adaptation."

"Enough!" Peter roars. "Analyze your errors in the void. Die!"

choice("Fight Peter together with the Overseer.", B3-Ch09b-Together, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09b-Together)
choice("Let the Overseer fight Peter while I dispel all of Peter's spells.", B3-Ch09b-Dispel, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09b-Dispel)


ID: B3-Ch09b-Dispel
TEXT:

Peter opens with a stone cage, thrusting his hands forward to box the drone within solid rock. The geometric signature is unmistakable from the first two hand positions; I reverse the flow in mid-cast, shattering the construct before the stone can knit. The drone surges forward, firing pressurized acid bolts that drive Peter into a rapid retreat.

I crack his second and third formations with quick reverse counters. Peter catches on instantly. He abandons complex incantations, stripping his arsenal down to raw, instantaneous projectile bursts that leave zero window for reverse framing. I track his hands, but the window is gone; basic dispelling against rapid-fire projectiles contributes nothing to the line.

I drop into a forward glide to engage with direct elemental spells.

Coarse hemp snaps tight around both my ankles. Before I can look down, two more bindings lash around my forearms, wrenching my wrists hard against my lower back.

Four conjured ropes anchor below my boots, tethered into empty air.

I locked my attention entirely on Peter's duel and left my blind spots open. He baited me with the drone skirmish while feeding conjuration lines from beneath. I wrench my arms against the cord, but the knots hold fast; without arm motion, I have no way to channel fire to burn through the fibers. My finger-flick repertoire doesn't cover binding releases.

"Overseer, assistance—"

"Time expired," Peter says, cutting me off.

A jagged arc of lightning cracks into my chest. The shock locks my breath, burns through my vision, and throws everything into black.

#if(v_b3_ch9_assassinate == 0) {
Voices drift through a heavy fog. Low, tight, edged with tension. I try to force my eyes open, but my limbs refuse to respond; paralysis holds every muscle rigid.

"We cannot leave them behind," the king says. "They will be torn apart out here."

"With respect, sire—why concern yourself with their fate?" Peter's voice is stiff. "They betrayed our movement. They stood with the Overseer. We owe them nothing."

"Their enmity in this hour does not erase the blood they spilled on our behalf," the king replies. "Wake them before we depart. Let them face whatever follows on their own feet."

"As you command, my lord," Peter says. "I will wake the girl only. The man may know the counter to my transit spell, and I will not take that risk."

A high vibrational hum builds and cuts off sharply. Silence settles for several long heartbeats—broken by hands grabbing my tunic, violently shaking me as Flower's screams pierce my ears.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_assassinate == 1) {
Voices drift through a heavy fog. Low, tight, edged with tension. I try to force my eyes open, but my limbs refuse to respond; paralysis holds every muscle rigid.

"We cannot leave them behind," the king says. "They will be torn apart out here."

"With respect, sire—why concern yourself with their fate?" Peter's voice is stiff. "They came with explicit intent to execute us. We owe them nothing."

"Their enmity in this hour does not erase the blood they spilled on our behalf," the king replies. "Wake them before we depart. Let them face whatever follows on their own feet."

"As you command, my lord," Peter says. "I will wake the girl only. The man may know the counter to my transit spell, and I will not take that risk."

A high vibrational hum builds and cuts off sharply. Silence settles for several long heartbeats—broken by hands grabbing my tunic, violently shaking me as Flower's screams pierce my ears.

}
