ID: B3-Ch09b-Together
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch9_element == 0) {
Peter opens with a stone cage, attempting to box the drone within solid rock. The machine reacts instantly, spraying high-yield acid across the pillars to dissolve the stone before it can set. I join the offensive, hurling rapid fire volleys from Flower's drills that force Peter onto his heels behind a layered barrier.

Just as our combined pressure fractures his guard, four semi-transparent walls lock around the drone, sealing it inside an impenetrable cube.

"Sire, do not strain your reserves!" Peter calls out to the king. "I have the situation contained!"

"Maintain your focus," the king replies, his voice strained as he deflects Flower's relentless firestorm. "A containment barrier of this scale requires minimal anchor. Finish Barry."

"Understood, sire." Peter turns, hands shaping the familiar freezing glyph toward the trapped drone.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_element == 1) {
Peter opens with a stone cage, attempting to box the drone within solid rock. The machine reacts instantly, spraying high-yield acid across the pillars to dissolve the stone before it can set. I join the offensive, hurling high-pressure water spears from Illuna's drills that force Peter onto his heels behind a layered barrier.

Just as our combined pressure fractures his guard, four semi-transparent walls lock around the drone, sealing it inside an impenetrable cube.

"Sire, do not strain your reserves!" Peter calls out to the king. "I have the situation contained!"

"Maintain your focus," the king replies, his voice strained as he deflects Flower's relentless firestorm. "A containment barrier of this scale requires minimal anchor. Finish Barry."

"Understood, sire." Peter turns, hands shaping the familiar freezing glyph toward the trapped drone.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_element == 2) {
Peter opens with a stone cage, attempting to box the drone within solid rock. The machine reacts instantly, spraying high-yield acid across the pillars to dissolve the stone before it can set. I join the offensive, hurling honed wind blades from Melindra's drills that force Peter onto his heels behind a layered barrier.

Just as our combined pressure fractures his guard, four semi-transparent walls lock around the drone, sealing it inside an impenetrable cube.

"Sire, do not strain your reserves!" Peter calls out to the king. "I have the situation contained!"

"Maintain your focus," the king replies, his voice strained as he deflects Flower's relentless firestorm. "A containment barrier of this scale requires minimal anchor. Finish Barry."

"Understood, sire." Peter turns, hands shaping the familiar freezing glyph toward the trapped drone.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_element == 3) {
Peter opens with a stone cage, attempting to box the drone within solid rock. The machine reacts instantly, spraying high-yield acid across the pillars to dissolve the stone before it can set. I join the offensive, hurling dense icicles from Kate's drills that force Peter onto his heels behind a layered barrier.

Just as our combined pressure fractures his guard, four semi-transparent walls lock around the drone, sealing it inside an impenetrable cube.

"Sire, do not strain your reserves!" Peter calls out to the king. "I have the situation contained!"

"Maintain your focus," the king replies, his voice strained as he deflects Flower's relentless firestorm. "A containment barrier of this scale requires minimal anchor. Finish Barry."

"Understood, sire." Peter turns, hands shaping the familiar freezing glyph toward the trapped drone.

}
I read the glyph before his fingers close the first loop. My hands trace the reverse sequence in fluid succession; the frost spell unravels into harmless vapor before reaching the barrier. The box remains intact, but the lethal freeze is dead.

"Overseer protocol is currently unable to breach containment or project force externally without engaging restricted routines," the drone hums from inside the glass-like walls. "The protocol apologizes for the inconvenience and trusts this development will not degrade your satisfaction score in the monthly review."

"Hold tight, Overseer," I say. "Any spell he throws into that box, I'll unpick."

Peter lowers his arms. He studies me in silence, the stillness stretching across several long heartbeats.

"What's the matter, Peter?" I ask. "Out of ideas?"

"No," Peter says quietly. "I simply underestimated you. In this realm, you operate as a full generalist. You deserve to be engaged as one. Tell me, Barry—did you ever witness a duel between Brinhorn and Azarius?"

"A fair few," I say. "Brinhorn insisted on my presence in the stands during his formal challenges while I studied under him."

"Studied under Brinh—" Peter cuts himself off, pinching the bridge of his nose with a sharp exhale. "No matter. Apprentice or bystander, the truth remains: a contest between two true generalists follows a discipline unlike any other mage duel. You recognize that structure?"

"Hard to miss," I say.

"Indeed," Peter murmurs. "I witnessed only one such match—travel permits beyond Varathia are near impossible for Ollendor citizens—but that single engagement is burned into my mind."

The white expanse tilts. A heavy wave of vertigo rolls through my skull, churning my stomach as if I'd downed a tankard of dwarven spirit in a single pull.

"As Brinhorn's former pupil, you will recognize every beat of the sequence I am about to deploy. Let us see if you can match your master's standard."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09b-Balance, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09b-Balance)


ID: B3-Ch09b-Manually
TEXT:

I bank hard around another cluster of fireballs. Through the trailing heat, Peter stands forty paces out, both arms outstretched, fingers twitching in rapid micro-adjustments as he steers the individual projectiles across the sky.

The homing formula is entirely autonomous; the caster weaves the targeting lock once and moves on to fresh spells. Manual adjustment makes no sense.

Unless the spell never survived the dispel at all.

He used ethereal matter shaping to sculpt fifty fiery constructs out of nothing, exactly the way Flower materialized the palm tree and the cart.

The dispel broke the original spell cleanly. He fabricated the replacement swarm to bluff me into fleeing instead of pressing the attack while his hands were tied steering fifty separate constructs.

Time to shatter the bluff.

I lure a cluster of fireballs wide into a sweeping arc, then whip around the formation into a dead sprint straight at Peter. My fingers trace a basic disorient hex—barely enough to rattle a novice's balance for three seconds.

I close the distance and loose the hex point-blank into his chest. Peter flinches, stumbling backward as his focus breaks.

The fifty fireballs pop and vanish instantly.

"I knew it," I say, planting my boots. "The dispel took. You were steering each one by hand. You really need more points in Bluff."

"You test my patience," Peter rasps, straightening up. "You do not want to see my actual repertoire in this realm. Yield, before this turns lethal."

"Was that an attempt at intimidation?" I ask. "Because it lacked conviction. Try again."

Peter raises both arms, drawing deep from his reserves as he weaves a wide-dispersion array: a freezing rain sequence designed to saturate the entire perimeter in incapacitating sub-zero droplets.

choice("Try making a roof out of stone and placing it over my head.", B3-Ch09b-Roof, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09b-Roof)
choice("Wait for him to finish casting the spell.", B3-Ch09b-Counter, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09b-Counter)
choice("Try to dispel the freezing rain before Peter finishes casting the spell.", B3-Ch09b-Freezing, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09b-Freezing)


ID: B3-Ch09b-Roof
TEXT:

I snap through the gestures to raise a thick slab of floating stone directly above my head, angling the surface to shed the incoming deluge.

The rock locks in place—and coarse cord snaps around both my ankles. A heartbeat later, two more bindings whip around my wrists, pinning my forearms behind my back.

Four conjured ropes anchor below my boots. Across the gap, the sky remains clear; the freezing rain never fell. The incantation was a complete feint.

I pull against the bindings, but the knots hold rigid against my wrists. Without arm clearance, basic fire channeling is locked out, and my finger-flick repertoire has nothing for heavy hemp.

"Time expired," Peter says.

A jagged arc of lightning cracks into my chest. The shock locks my breath, burns through my vision, and throws everything into black.

#if(v_b3_ch9_assassinate == 0) {
Voices drift through a heavy fog. Low, tight, edged with tension. I try to force my eyes open, but my limbs refuse to respond; paralysis holds every muscle rigid.

"We cannot leave them behind," the king says. "They will be torn apart out here."

"With respect, sire—why concern yourself with their fate?" Peter's voice is stiff. "They betrayed our movement. They stood with the Overseer. We owe them nothing."

"Their enmity in this hour does not erase the blood they spilled on our behalf," the king replies. "Wake them before we depart. Let them face whatever follows on their own feet."

"As you command, my lord," Peter says. "I will wake the girl only. The man may know the counter to my transit spell, and I will not take that risk."

A high vibrational hum builds and cuts off sharply. Silence settles for several long heartbeats—broken by hands grabbing my tunic, violently shaking me as Flower's screams pierce my ears.

}
