ID: B3-Ch09b-Counter
TEXT:

I hold my ground and hold my hands still. The posture is a gamble, but something about his posture smells like a trap.

Peter weaves the complex freezing rain array with wide, theatrical sweeps, fixing his gaze on my chest—except every third movement, his eyes dart downward toward my blind spot.

I track the line of his glance. Four thick hemp cords snake upward from the void below, creeping toward my boots.

"Nice try!" I snap through a two-step fire sequence, sending a wide fan of flames sweeping under my boots to reduce all four ropes to falling ash. "Staging a high-tier weather array just to slip ropes under my feet? You really take me for an amateur."

Peter drops the rain glyph entirely.

"Hardly," he admits, folding his hands. "Though I hoped for a few seconds of hesitation. I underestimated you. In this realm, you operate as a true generalist. You deserve to be engaged as one. Tell me, Barry—did you ever witness a duel between Brinhorn and Azarius?"

"A fair few," I say. "Brinhorn insisted on my presence in the stands during his formal challenges while I studied under him."

"Studied under Brinh—" Peter cuts himself off, pinching the bridge of his nose with a sharp exhale. "No matter. Apprentice or bystander, the truth remains: a contest between two true generalists follows a discipline unlike any other mage duel. You recognize that structure?"

"Hard to miss," I say.

"Indeed," Peter murmurs. "I witnessed only one such match—travel permits beyond Varathia are near impossible for Ollendor citizens—but that single engagement is burned into my mind."

The white expanse tilts. A heavy wave of vertigo rolls through my skull, churning my stomach as if I'd downed a tankard of dwarven spirit in a single pull.

"As Brinhorn's former pupil, you will recognize every beat of the sequence I am about to deploy. Let us see if you can match your master's standard."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09b-Balance, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09b-Balance)


ID: B3-Ch09b-Balance
TEXT:

My inner ears scream. The horizon spins like a roulette wheel, bile surging into my throat. Pure immaterial vertigo—I had no idea bodiless consciousness could suffer motion sickness this vile.

Peter gave away his setup. I know the exact counter-sequence Brinhorn used to pull himself out of this identical trap in the third challenge.

I ignite twin tongues of fire across my palms and press both hands flat against my cheeks. Seared flesh sizzles, blinding agony tearing through my temples. Gritting my teeth through the burn, I conjure two solid granite boulders on either side of my head and slam them inward simultaneously, smashing directly into the fresh burns.

The double concussive shock shatters the inner-ear loop. I summon a tub of freezing mountain water overhead and drop it straight onto my crown, icy water drenching me from head to foot.

Teeth rattling from the shock, I level my right index finger at Peter's face and spin it counter-clockwise, willing the unseen acoustic wards from his ears to shatter.

Peter claps slowly, open admiration cutting through his reserve as I fight off violent tremors. "Incredible. I threw that challenge out in jest, but you committed down to the letter. Every brutal beat of Brinhorn's opening counter, down to the rare dispelling variant he used to strip Azarius's sound wards."

"W-well, I wrote the counter-f-form for him, after all!" I chatter through numb lips, trying to keep my stance upright.

"I doubted you would execute it, even knowing the theory," Peter says. "Watching someone inflict that level of deliberate violence on himself without blinking makes me wonder if you caught Brinhorn's madness, or if you infected him with yours."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09b-Versatile, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09b-Versatile)


ID: B3-Ch09b-Versatile
TEXT:

"Not like there was a gentle alternative," I say, the shaking finally easing as the shock settles. "That high-frequency resonance directly disrupts inner-ear fluid balance. Unpicking it cleanly takes a master white mage ten minutes of delicate threadwork. That's why Azarius leaned on sonic arrays so heavily—versatile, near-impossible to counter on the fly. Brinhorn was a pain in the neck to live with, but borrowing the dwarven hangover cure to short-circuit the nerve response was pure practical genius."

"It was magnificent," Peter says, a wistful note in his voice. "Absurd on paper, yet ruthlessly efficient. Why waste minutes untangling acoustic pressure waves when several hundred years of dwarven tavern brawling offer an immediate reset? A masterclass in combat logic."

#if(v_b3_ch9_backup == 0) {
"Delighted by your mutual appreciation," the king's voice bellows from across the glow, where he strains to keep layered barriers intact against Flower's relentless flanking volleys. "Now finish him, Peter! My aura on this plane barely holds this girl. You cannot handle both of them at once!"

"Right away, my king," Peter calls back. "I will close the engagement."

"How, exactly?" I ask. "Your acoustic earplug trick won't land twice. Try that whistle again and you'll need the cold bucket yourself."

"Have you forgotten the premise?" Peter asks, fingers curling into fresh glyphs. "We have barely concluded the opening exchange. The real duel begins now."

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_backup == 1) {
"Delighted by your mutual appreciation," the king's voice bellows from across the glow, where he strains to keep layered barriers intact against Flower's relentless flanking volleys. "Now finish him, Peter! My aura on this plane barely holds this girl. You cannot handle Barry, Flower, and the Overseer simultaneously!"

"Right away, my king," Peter calls back. "I will close the engagement."

"How, exactly?" I ask. "Your acoustic earplug trick won't land twice. Try that whistle again and you'll need the cold bucket yourself."

"Have you forgotten the premise?" Peter asks, fingers curling into fresh glyphs. "We have barely concluded the opening exchange. The real duel begins now."

}
"You watched me smash two boulders into my own skull to match his play and you still want to press it?" I say. "I can't tell whether that's courage or sheer stubbornness."

"And I wonder at your confidence," Peter replies, "given that you know who claimed victory in every single duel those two ever fought."

"Reenactments allow for improvisation," I say. "You'll see soon enough."

"Show me," Peter says, and launches into Azarius's signature opening.

choice("Counter him by doing exactly what Brinhorn did in the battle against Azarius.", B3-Ch09b-Copy, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09b-Copy)
choice("Use my own spells instead of copying Brinhorn, in order to throw Peter off.", B3-Ch09b-Adversary, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09b-Adversary)
choice("Begin by copying Brinhorn's spells, but switch to my own spells later on.", B3-Ch09b-Guarantee, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09b-Guarantee)


ID: B3-Ch09b-Adversary
TEXT:

A hundred winged manticores fill the sky, wheeling in tight, overlapping orbits from every angle, including the space directly beneath my boots. In the historical match, the overwhelming majority were phantasms; only five were flesh and blood, with Azarius himself concealed among the swarm in beast form, waiting for the killing stroke.

Peter matched the opening down to the second—layering illusion, summoning, and shapeshifting without a single telltale gesture. He must have threaded the anchors while keeping my attention locked on the banter.

The manticores weave and cross, shuffling their positions in endless spirals. Identifying the five real bodies becomes harder by the second. Exactly like Azarius, Peter takes his time; every second I spend hesitating frays my certainty.

Copying Brinhorn's textbook defense means stepping into a counter Peter has already studied. If I want the initiative, I have to break the pattern.

I kick into my air currents and plunge straight into the swarm, homing in on the manticore trailing Peter's aura signature. A beast lunges to intercept; I roll beneath its claws and keep driving forward.

Close the distance, drop a paralysis bind before he drops the form, and the duel ends. Shapeshifting transitions leave the caster wide open.

Thirty paces. Twenty. Ten.

The manticore image flickers and dissolves. In its place stands Peter in human form—hands already locked in a paralysis grip.

He never shifted at all. Pure phantom projection.

The spell strikes my chest before my fingers can form a counter-geometry. My limbs lock into stone. Before I can force mana through my throat to dispel the bind, a wave of magical slumber washes over my mind, dragging me under.

#if(v_b3_ch9_assassinate == 0) {
Voices drift through a heavy fog. Low, tight, edged with tension. I try to force my eyes open, but my limbs refuse to respond; paralysis holds every muscle rigid.

"We cannot leave them behind," the king says. "They will be torn apart out here."

"With respect, sire—why concern yourself with their fate?" Peter's voice is stiff. "They betrayed our movement. They stood with the Overseer. We owe them nothing."

"Their enmity in this hour does not erase the blood they spilled on our behalf," the king replies. "Wake them before we depart. Let them face whatever follows on their own feet."

"As you command, my lord," Peter says. "I will wake the girl only. The man may know the counter to my transit spell, and I will not take that risk."

A high vibrational hum builds and cuts off sharply. Silence settles for several long heartbeats—broken by hands grabbing my tunic, violently shaking me as Flower's screams pierce my ears.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_assassinate == 1) {
