ID: B3-Ch09b-Copy
TEXT:

A hundred winged manticores fill the sky, wheeling in tight, overlapping orbits from every angle, including the space directly beneath my boots. In the historical match, the overwhelming majority were phantasms; only five were flesh and blood, with Azarius himself concealed among the swarm in beast form, waiting for the killing stroke.

Peter matched the opening down to the second—layering illusion, summoning, and shapeshifting without a single telltale gesture. He must have threaded the anchors while keeping my attention locked on the banter.

The manticores weave and cross, shuffling their positions in endless spirals. Identifying the five real bodies becomes harder by the second. Exactly like Azarius, Peter takes his time; every second I spend hesitating frays my certainty.

Brinhorn's counter-sequence is intricate, but the mechanics are etched into my memory. Time to test the flow.

While the swarm circles, I trace micro-currents through my fingers, feeling for the resonance frequencies I logged years ago. The particle streams lock into alignment, responding cleanly to my aura.

I weave the sensory enhancement charm—the spell equivalent of the Observation stat from the device I left behind in the flesh. My visual acuity sharpens instantly. Five manticores stand out from the pack, their wingbeats fighting real air resistance while the illusions glide on programmed paths. The heightened focus locks onto Peter's disguised form among them.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09b-Mistake, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09b-Mistake)


ID: B3-Ch09b-Mistake
TEXT:

With the five real beasts tagged, the board simplifies.

Brinhorn handled the engagement cleanly right up until the final transition. Matching his script up to the pivot point is sound tactics. Next in sequence: a localized reflex acceleration charm. I begin the hand motions—

The manticore carrying Peter's signature folds its wings and dives straight for me.

Azarius never broke formation like that. Diving in beast form leaves him wide open; shapeshifting back to human takes two full seconds. If he wants to brawl in beast shape, I'll meet him with a point-blank paralysis charm.

I drop into stance, timing the approach.

Twenty paces. Ten. Five.

I loose the paralysis bolt—and the manticore shatters like glass. Peter stands three feet to the left in full human form, perfectly positioned outside my line of fire. He never shifted; the dive was another projected phantom.

My bolt snaps into empty air. Before I can recover my balance, Peter's counter-paralysis hits my breastbone, followed instantly by a heavy sleep charm that drags my vision into darkness.

#if(v_b3_ch9_assassinate == 0) {
Voices drift through a heavy fog. Low, tight, edged with tension. I try to force my eyes open, but my limbs refuse to respond; paralysis holds every muscle rigid.

"We cannot leave them behind," the king says. "They will be torn apart out here."

"With respect, sire—why concern yourself with their fate?" Peter's voice is stiff. "They betrayed our movement. They stood with the Overseer. We owe them nothing."

"Their enmity in this hour does not erase the blood they spilled on our behalf," the king replies. "Wake them before we depart. Let them face whatever follows on their own feet."

"As you command, my lord," Peter says. "I will wake the girl only. The man may know the counter to my transit spell, and I will not take that risk."

A high vibrational hum builds and cuts off sharply. Silence settles for several long heartbeats—broken by hands grabbing my tunic, violently shaking me as Flower's screams pierce my ears.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_assassinate == 1) {
Voices drift through a heavy fog. Low, tight, edged with tension. I try to force my eyes open, but my limbs refuse to respond; paralysis holds every muscle rigid.

"We cannot leave them behind," the king says. "They will be torn apart out here."

"With respect, sire—why concern yourself with their fate?" Peter's voice is stiff. "They came with explicit intent to execute us. We owe them nothing."

"Their enmity in this hour does not erase the blood they spilled on our behalf," the king replies. "Wake them before we depart. Let them face whatever follows on their own feet."

"As you command, my lord," Peter says. "I will wake the girl only. The man may know the counter to my transit spell, and I will not take that risk."

A high vibrational hum builds and cuts off sharply. Silence settles for several long heartbeats—broken by hands grabbing my tunic, violently shaking me as Flower's screams pierce my ears.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch09b-Melding, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09b-Melding, v_ac_b3_ch9_consolation = +1)


ID: B3-Ch09b-Guarantee
TEXT:

A hundred winged manticores fill the sky, wheeling in tight, overlapping orbits from every angle, including the space directly beneath my boots. In the historical match, the overwhelming majority were phantasms; only five were flesh and blood, with Azarius himself concealed among the swarm in beast form, waiting for the killing stroke.

Peter matched the opening down to the second—layering illusion, summoning, and shapeshifting without a single telltale gesture. He must have threaded the anchors while keeping my attention locked on the banter.

The manticores weave and cross, shuffling their positions in endless spirals. Identifying the five real bodies becomes harder by the second. Exactly like Azarius, Peter takes his time; every second I spend hesitating frays my certainty.

Brinhorn's counter-sequence is intricate, but the mechanics are etched into my memory. Time to test the flow.

While the swarm circles, I trace micro-currents through my fingers, feeling for the resonance frequencies I logged years ago. The particle streams lock into alignment, responding cleanly to my aura.

I weave the sensory enhancement charm—the spell equivalent of the Observation stat from the device I left behind in the flesh. My visual acuity sharpens instantly. Five manticores stand out from the pack, their wingbeats fighting real air resistance while the illusions glide on programmed paths. The heightened focus locks onto Peter's disguised form among them.

With the five real beasts tagged, the board simplifies.

The question is when to break script. Peter has copied the historical match move for move, but he won't hold it to the finish; he'll pivot the moment he senses an advantage.

The manticore bearing Peter's signature tucks its wings and dives straight for my throat.

Azarius never broke line like that.

Shapeshifting back to human mid-flight leaves him open for two seconds. Diving in beast shape means he wants me to commit to a ground defense. It's bait. He wants me to cast first so he can punish the recovery.

I drop into a low stance, deliberately feigning a short-range paralysis setup to draw him in.

The manticore image dissolves ten paces out. Peter stands in human form, fingers already locked in a paralysis release.

I ignite my air currents and throw myself sideways in a sharp banking roll. His bolt snaps through empty air where my chest was a fraction of a second earlier.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09b-Improvisations, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09b-Improvisations)


ID: B3-Ch09b-Improvisations
TEXT:

I clear his line of fire, but the sudden evasive dive breaks my sensory focus.

Two manticores plunge from above, claws slashing through the air inches from my shoulders. I spin clear, only for four more to dive in from the flank, the remaining hundred swirling into an aggressive descending funnel.

The sensory charm is dead; I can't distinguish the five real beasts from the phantoms anymore. Time for historical reenactments is over.

#if(v_b3_ch9_element == 0) {
As the swarm converges, I unleash a massive radial wave of fire across the perimeter. The phantoms flicker harmlessly through the blaze, but the five living manticores ignite, screeching as the flames consume them. Their charred carcasses drift motionless in the low gravity.

Flower's wide-dispersion drills pay off.

Peter floats behind a thick slab of reinforced granite, unharmed. Before he can weave a fresh summoning array, I rain a relentless barrage of fire arrows against his barrier, cracking the stone and forcing him to abandon his position. Instead of falling back, Peter counter-attacks, unleashing a dense, swirling cloud of dark green poison mist that engulfs us both before I can bank away.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_element == 1) {
As the swarm converges, I conjure twin water whips, snapping them in high-velocity concentric sweeps that shred everything within thirty paces. The phantoms pass through the arcs without effect, but the five living manticores are sliced into falling ribbons, their remains drifting in the low gravity.

Illuna's wide-dispersion drills pay off.

Peter floats behind a thick slab of reinforced granite, unharmed. Before he can weave a fresh summoning array, I rain a relentless barrage of pressurized water lances against his barrier, cracking the stone and forcing him to abandon his position. Instead of falling back, Peter counter-attacks, unleashing a dense, swirling cloud of dark green poison mist that engulfs us both before I can bank away.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_element == 2) {
As the swarm converges, I release a tempest of lethal wind blades in every direction. The phantoms pass through the gale unaffected, but the five living manticores are torn to shreds, their remains drifting in the low gravity.

Melindra's wide-dispersion drills pay off.

Peter floats behind a thick slab of reinforced granite, unharmed. Before he can weave a fresh summoning array, I rain a relentless barrage of slicing wind blades against his barrier, cracking the stone and forcing him to abandon his position. Instead of falling back, Peter counter-attacks, unleashing a dense, swirling cloud of dark green poison mist that engulfs us both before I can bank away.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_element == 3) {
As the swarm converges, I release a freezing shockwave across the perimeter. The phantoms pass through the frost unaffected, but the five living manticores freeze solid, drifting as rigid ice statues in the low gravity.

Kate's wide-dispersion drills pay off.

Peter floats behind a thick slab of reinforced granite, unharmed. Before he can weave a fresh summoning array, I rain a relentless barrage of jagged icicles against his barrier, cracking the stone and forcing him to abandon his position. Instead of falling back, Peter counter-attacks, unleashing a dense, swirling cloud of dark green poison mist that engulfs us both before I can bank away.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch09b-Poison, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09b-Poison)


ID: B3-Ch09b-Poison
TEXT:

The anti-poison tincture I swallowed on the material plane has zero presence here. The toxin takes hold in an instant: a spike of pain hammers through my skull, and warm blood splatters against my lips. It falls short of the agonizing cranial pressure from the botched teleportation with Leila, but it shreds my concentration down to raw survival.

Poison, of all things. If Illuna were here, she would never let me live it down.

I sweep an arm out, summoning a hard gust of wind to scatter the toxic cloud. The damage is done, but the air clears.

Peter is nowhere in sight.

"A simple diversion, yet surprisingly effective," Peter's voice drifts from empty air ten paces to my left. "What manner of mage enters a duel without basic toxin wards? Perhaps I gave you too much credit."

"Hiding behind invisibility?" I spit blood into the glow. "You think you're Albert? You're not half as skilled at suppressing your aura. I can pinpoint your coordinates without a single stat boost from my device."

"You know theory, but you know nothing of venom," Peter says from the void. "Time works against you. The toxin systematically dismantles each sensory channel in turn: magical sense degrades first, followed by smell, then sight. I offer this counsel out of respect for a fellow generalist. If you hold a final technique in reserve, deploy it now. Do not waste what remains."

choice("Don't let Peter's taunts get to me and wait for him to make his move.", B3-Ch09b-Appreciate, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09b-Appreciate)
choice("Fight Peter now with everything I've got.", B3-Ch09b-Struggle, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09b-Struggle)


ID: B3-Ch09b-Struggle
TEXT:

If this is the final exchange, I'm throwing everything into it.

Fighting through the throbbing pressure behind my eyes, I weave three overlapping arrays simultaneously—a multi-tier formulation engineered to detonate in a wide spray of rapid-setting adhesive.

Mid-sequence, a wave of violent nausea doubles me over. The particle flow fractures, collapsing the entire array into useless static.

"An ambitious construct, I assume," Peter's voice murmurs from the air. "I was foolish to exercise such caution. Rest now."

"Wait—"

A wave of magical slumber washes over my mind, dragging my consciousness down into the dark.

#if(v_b3_ch9_assassinate == 0) {
Voices drift through a heavy fog. Low, tight, edged with tension. I try to force my eyes open, but my limbs refuse to respond; paralysis holds every muscle rigid.

"We cannot leave them behind," the king says. "They will be torn apart out here."

"With respect, sire—why concern yourself with their fate?" Peter's voice is stiff. "They betrayed our movement. They stood with the Overseer. We owe them nothing."

"Their enmity in this hour does not erase the blood they spilled on our behalf," the king replies. "Wake them before we depart. Let them face whatever follows on their own feet."

"As you command, my lord," Peter says. "I will wake the girl only. The man may know the counter to my transit spell, and I will not take that risk."

A high vibrational hum builds and cuts off sharply. Silence settles for several long heartbeats—broken by hands grabbing my tunic, violently shaking me as Flower's screams pierce my ears.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_assassinate == 1) {
Voices drift through a heavy fog. Low, tight, edged with tension. I try to force my eyes open, but my limbs refuse to respond; paralysis holds every muscle rigid.

"We cannot leave them behind," the king says. "They will be torn apart out here."

"With respect, sire—why concern yourself with their fate?" Peter's voice is stiff. "They came with explicit intent to execute us. We owe them nothing."

"Their enmity in this hour does not erase the blood they spilled on our behalf," the king replies. "Wake them before we depart. Let them face whatever follows on their own feet."

"As you command, my lord," Peter says. "I will wake the girl only. The man may know the counter to my transit spell, and I will not take that risk."

A high vibrational hum builds and cuts off sharply. Silence settles for several long heartbeats—broken by hands grabbing my tunic, violently shaking me as Flower's screams pierce my ears.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch09b-Melding, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09b-Melding, v_ac_b3_ch9_consolation = +1)


ID: B3-Ch09b-Appreciate
TEXT:

"Appreciate the tactical advice, Peter," I say, steadying my breathing. "Reminding me of my reserve repertoire was remarkably generous. I might have let it slip."

"If you intend to strike, do it now," Peter replies from the void. "Soon, your magical sense will fail entirely."

"I think I'll hold the card," I say. "Keep you guessing. The tension suits you."

Silence answers from the empty space. His insistence on prodding me into an immediate offensive is a dead tell. If the toxin genuinely intensified over time, he would stay invisible and let the poison do the work. He wants me to burn my reserves because the formulation is temporary.

Even now, the throbbing behind my temples recedes.

"Gods above—you weren't lying!" I yell, putting panic into my voice. "The spread is accelerating! What did you lace this with, you bastard?"

"I gave fair warning," Peter's voice carries a thin edge of hesitation. "Unleash your technique before—"

"What happened to your aura?" I shout, clutching my head theatrically. "Show yourself! I thought this was an honorable duel!"

"What are you screaming about?" Peter demands. "The degradation rate cannot possibly—"

He cuts off. The silence hangs heavy.

"You baited me," he says flatly. "You bought time for the metabolic purge."

"Naturally," I say, lowering my hands as the headache clears entirely. "And now that we're both in fighting trim, we can resume our contest. Proper generalist protocol. Agreed?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09b-Puddle, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09b-Puddle, v_b3_ch3_stats_unavailable = 0)


ID: B3-Ch09b-Puddle
TEXT:

A violent wrench tears through my perception. My consciousness jerks through a disorienting corridor, oscillating violently between two realities.

One heartbeat, I lie face-down in a cold mud puddle in the storm-swept plaza of Ollendor; the next, I float suspended in the incandescent radiance of the ether.

Back and forth the perspective whips, violently wrenching my awareness until the surrounding light fuses directly with my thoughts, resounding with absolute clarity:

'This is not the place to resolve your conflict. Return to your sanctuaries now, and disturb these resting grounds no longer.'

The voice echoes within my own mind. Across the gap, the king and Peter freeze in place, eyes wide with alarm.

"Peter, did you hear that?" the king asks, looking around the empty glow. "Who speaks? The ether itself?"

"I... cannot say," Peter whispers, his posture tense.

choice("Attack Peter while he's still distracted.", B3-Ch09b-Illusory, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09b-Illusory, v_b3_ch9_listen = 0)
choice("Listen to the voice in my head and don't cast any more spells for the time being.", B3-Ch09b-Risky, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09b-Risky, v_b3_ch9_listen = 1)


ID: B3-Ch09b-Risky
TEXT:

set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_b3_ch9_assassinate == 0 && v_aura_hardening < 4 || v_b3_ch9_assassinate == 1 && v_aura_hardening < 4)
set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_aura_hardening >= 4)

"Until we identify the origin of that voice, continued combat here carries unacceptable risk," the king says.

"I agree, sire," Peter replies. He exhales slowly, turning toward me. "I took no pleasure in this course, but you leave no alternative."

"Alternative to what?"

A blinding jolt of high-voltage current tears through my earthen flesh in the plaza puddle.

"If this realm denies our battle, I will conclude it in the material world," Peter says in the ether, while his physical body channels lightning into my prone form below.

#if(v_aura_hardening < 4) {
The raw voltage overpowers my nerves. Agony locks my lungs, and my awareness collapses into black across both planes simultaneously.

"Of all the low-down... dirty tricks..."

}
#if(v_aura_hardening >= 4) {
My hardened aura absorbs the brunt of the electrical surge, blunting the lethal spike to a dull ache. He won't release the channel until he sees me drop, so I let my limbs go slack, rolling my eyes back and slumping into the mud on both planes at once.

"Is he down?" the king asks across the ethereal expanse.

"Neutralized," Peter says, turning his back to me as he cuts the current. "I felt his physical form stir below; extinguishing his consciousness on the earthen plane drops the ethereal projection."

"Well done," the king says. "One fewer threat. Now—"

I snap through the lightning sequence with minimal finger twitches.

A jagged bolt cracks from my outstretched hand, blasting directly into Peter's spine.

"What..." Peter gasps, his body seizing as his eyes roll up. "How... still awake..."

He collapses into the white glow, his physical form slumping into the puddle below.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_assassinate == 0 && v_aura_hardening < 4) {
Voices drift through a heavy fog. Low, tight, edged with tension. I try to force my eyes open, but my limbs refuse to respond; paralysis holds every muscle rigid.

"We cannot leave them behind," the king says. "They will be torn apart out here."

"With respect, sire—why concern yourself with their fate?" Peter's voice is stiff. "They betrayed our movement. They stood with the Overseer. We owe them nothing."

"Their enmity in this hour does not erase the blood they spilled on our behalf," the king replies. "Wake them before we depart. Let them face whatever follows on their own feet."

"As you command, my lord," Peter says. "I will wake the girl only. The man may know the counter to my transit spell, and I will not take that risk."

A high vibrational hum builds and cuts off sharply. Silence settles for several long heartbeats—broken by hands grabbing my tunic, violently shaking me as Flower's screams pierce my ears.

}
