ID: B3-Ch09b-Kill
TEXT:

They are active combatants. Leaving them breathing is an invitation to a rematch we might lose.

"Phew!" Flower glides down on fire jets, wiping her brow. "That was way too close. Nice work, Barry!"

I don't answer. I weave two massive slabs of granite into the air six feet on either side of the king's head. With a matching gesture, I summon a second pair on either side of Peter.

"Barry?" Flower lands lightly, looking between the stones. "What are the rocks for?"

I raise both hands, gripping the mana anchors of all four slabs at once.

Before she can step between us, I slam my palms together.

The granite slabs crash inward with thunderous force, obliterating both skulls in a spray of thick red paste.

"NO!" Flower screams, lunging forward.

She skids to a halt beside the corpses, staring in frozen horror at the crushed remains.

"You killed them..." Her voice shakes. "Why?"

"They were too dangerous to leave behind," I say. "There was no other way."

"No other way?" Flower's eyes flash with fury. "They were helpless! We could have held them!"

"Held them how?" I ask. "Both of them cast without moving an arm. Rope wouldn't hold them ten seconds."

"The Overseer has anti-magic cells!" Flower yells. "That was the whole point of making a deal with him!"

"Too much can go wrong in transit," I say flatly. "One slip and we're fighting them on the street. Dead men don't break out."

#if(v_b3_ch9_backup == 0) {
"And what will you say to Daren?" Flower asks, her voice dropping. "We gave him our word we'd keep casualties down."

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_backup == 1) {
"The Overseer protocol notes displeasure with this unilateral action," the drone states. "Both subjects represented significant intelligence assets. Refrain from unsanctioned executions to avoid compromising our operational agreement."

"Noted," I say.

"And what will you say to Daren?" Flower asks, her voice dropping. "We gave him our word we'd keep casualties down."

}
choice(""I'll tell him the truth."", B3-Ch09b-Cube, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09b-Cube, v_b3_ch9_enemies = 2)
choice(""I'll tell him that there was no way to avoid killing them, and that they died in battle."", B3-Ch09b-Cube, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09b-Cube, v_b3_ch9_enemies = 3)


ID: B3-Ch09b-Cube
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch9_enemies == 2) {
"I'll tell him the truth."

"You're going to tell him you crushed their heads while they lay unconscious?" Flower asks, staring at me. "How do you think he'll take that?"

"They were only unconscious because we put them there," I say. "We barely survived this exchange out here where we had every advantage. We couldn't afford a rematch on the material plane. Daren will see reason."

"Fine," Flower says quietly, looking away. "I'm not arguing with you. I've had this exact debate with Petal too many times to waste my breath. If that's how you see people, nothing I say will change it."

She looks toward the horizon.

"We need to get you back to your sanctuary before the ether expels us. Do you know the path?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_enemies == 3) {
"I'll tell him that there was no way to avoid killing them, and that they died in battle."

"You want to lie to Daren?" Flower's voice rises. "Barry, no."

"It's not a complete fabrication," I say. "The margin was razor-thin. If the exchange tilted slightly differently, they would have died in the crossfire anyway."

"Except it didn't tilt differently!" Flower snaps. "The fight was over! They were helpless on the ground and you murdered them!"

"They were only helpless because we put them there," I counter. "We barely survived this exchange out here where we had every advantage. We couldn't afford a rematch on the material plane."

Flower lets out a heavy breath.

"Fine, Barry. Have it your way. I've had this debate with Petal too many times to waste my breath. If that's how you see people, nothing I say will change it."

She looks toward the horizon.

"We need to get you back to your sanctuary before the ether expels us. Do you know the path?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_backup == 0 && v_b3_ch9_enemies == 0) {
They fought beside us until hours ago. Ending them while they lie defenseless is a line I won't cross unless every alternative fails.

"Phew!" Flower glides down on fire jets, wiping her brow. "That was way too close. Nice work, Barry!"

"Thanks," I say, shaking off the tension. "You too."

I look down at the two unconscious figures floating in the glow.

"Any thoughts on what we do with them? Tying them up is useless—they cast without moving a finger."

"Couldn't the Overseer lock them in anti-magic cells on the earthen plane?" Flower suggests. "His drones can transport them."

"Sensible plan," I say. "I'll arrange it the moment we're back in the flesh. Are we clear to move?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_backup == 1 && v_b3_ch9_enemies == 0) {
They fought beside us until hours ago. Ending them while they lie defenseless is a line I won't cross unless every alternative fails.

"Phew!" Flower glides down on fire jets, wiping her brow. "That was way too close. Nice work, Barry!"

"Thanks," I say, shaking off the tension. "You too."

I look down at the two unconscious figures floating in the glow.

"Any thoughts on what we do with them? Tying them up is useless—they cast without moving a finger."

"Couldn't the Overseer lock them in anti-magic cells on the earthen plane?" Flower suggests. "His drones can transport them."

"Affirmative," the Overseer says. "Both subjects will be remanded to anti-magic paralysis containment once surface units re-establish local grid control."

"Problem solved," I say. "Are we clear to move?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_enemies == 0) {
"Nothing more to do out here," Flower says. "Let's find your sanctuary before the ether loses its temper. Do you remember the layout?"

"Vaguely," I say. "Nothing like the house you built with Illuna—no rooms, no paintings. Just an empty cube. Barely enough room to stand. Before the Overseer knocked me out, I was sitting with my knees tucked and my back against the wall."

"A cube..." Flower smiles faintly. "That takes me back. I lived in a cube when I was three, before I learned to shape the interior. Your memories match standard soul containment. Do you feel the direction?"

"There's a distinct pull," I say. "Like an invisible cord tugging on my chest."

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_enemies != 0) {
"Vaguely," I say. "Nothing like the house you built with Illuna—no rooms, no paintings. Just an empty cube. Barely enough room to stand. Before the Overseer knocked me out, I was sitting with my knees tucked and my back against the wall."

"A cube..." Flower smiles faintly. "That takes me back. I lived in a cube when I was three, before I learned to shape the interior. Your memories match standard soul containment. Do you feel the direction?"

"There's a distinct pull," I say. "Like an invisible cord tugging on my chest."

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_backup == 0) {
"The stronger the tension, the closer the cube," Flower says. "How hard is it pulling?"

"Like a small dog on a leash," I say, pointing toward an empty patch of radiance. "That heading."

"Fifty miles out, give or take," Flower calculates. "I'll jump us closer. Then we can gauge whether we fly the rest."

"Ready," I say.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_backup == 1) {
