"The Overseer protocol assumes jurisdiction," a descending drone announces near my shoulder. "Thank you for your cooperation. For alliance debriefings or municipal planning consultations, a green-eyed liaison awaits within the palace. Have a pleasant day."

"Where are you taking them?" I ask, but the drone lifts off into the rain without responding.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch09b-Agreement, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09b-Agreement)


ID: B3-Ch09b-Agreement
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch9_premonition != 2) {
The drones work with ruthless efficiency. Within five minutes, the square is clear of bodies. By the time the last transport lifts off, Flower finishes guiding our companions' souls back to their sanctuaries, and one by one they stir in the rain, gathering around Illuna and me.

"The drone ordered us to the royal palace?" Daren asks, wiping rainwater from his brow as I finish the debrief.

"Clear instructions," I tell him. "If we want terms with the Overseer, we walk up to the throne room."

"A palace summons was never part of our agreement," Daren says, frowning.

"We never explicitly barred it either," I say. "Technically, he hasn't breached the letter."

"He could have easily sent a drone liaison here," Daren says, his hand resting on his pommel. "Summoning us to his seat is an assertion of authority."

"Can you blame him?" I ask. "He holds all the leverage. The rebel leadership is broken, the guard is shattered, and we are the only armed variable left in Ollendor."

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_premonition == 2) {
The drones work with ruthless efficiency. Within five minutes, the square is clear of bodies. By the time the last transport lifts off, Flower finishes guiding our companions' souls back to their sanctuaries, and one by one they stir in the rain, gathering around Illuna and me.

"The drone ordered us to the royal palace?" Daren asks, wiping rainwater from his brow as I finish the debrief, while Leila helps Kate to her feet nearby.

"Clear instructions," I tell him. "If we want terms with the Overseer, we walk up to the throne room."

"A palace summons was never part of our agreement," Daren says, frowning.

"We never explicitly barred it either," I say. "Technically, he hasn't breached the letter."

"He could have easily sent a drone liaison here," Daren says, his hand resting on his pommel. "Summoning us to his seat is an assertion of authority."

"Can you blame him?" I ask. "He holds all the leverage. The rebel leadership is broken, the guard is shattered, and we are the only armed variable left in Ollendor."

}
"Precisely," Daren mutters. "That is what concerns me."

"Currents are set," Melindra calls out.

"Thanks, Melindra!" Flower's voice pipes up as air currents lift her boots off the mud. "Petal wants to minimize physical strain on my limbs until I learn how to put out the red fire in my eyes."

"Don't worry about it," Melindra says, guiding the updraft. "I'll ferry you to the gates."

"Please," Flower says. "I'm still steering rebel souls back to their minds in the ether, so I can't drop the form just yet."

Hadrik pauses, scratching his beard. "...You're helping the rebels?"

"I can't leave them to drift out there!" Flower protests.

#if(v_b3_ch9_enemies == 0) {
"Leave Albert, Peter, and the king for last," Daren instructs. "We need the Overseer's drones to lock them into anti-magic cells before they regain consciousness. A rematch on the surface is a disaster we cannot afford. Especially against the king."

"The king and Peter can navigate their own way once they stir, but I'll hold Albert for the end," Flower promises.

"Good," Daren says, looking across the group. "Is everyone ready?"

"Aye!" Hadrik's voice booms over the wind.

"Then we move," Daren says, taking the lead. "Target: the palace of Ollendor."

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_enemies != 0) {
"Leave Albert, Peter, and the king for last," Daren instructs. "We need the Overseer's drones to lock them into anti-magic cells before they regain consciousness. A rematch on the surface is a disaster we cannot afford. Especially against the king."

"Right... the king," Flower says, letting out a tight, nervous laugh.

"Is everyone ready?" Daren asks, missing the tremor in her voice.

"Aye!" Hadrik's voice booms over the wind.

"Then we move," Daren says, taking the lead. "Target: the palace of Ollendor."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch09b-Fluke, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09b-Fluke)


ID: B3-Ch09b-Fluke
TEXT:

I turn to follow the column—and the world jerks.

For a fractured heartbeat, the dark streets of Ollendor vanish. My awareness snaps into the featureless white stone of my cube sanctuary, sitting cross-legged with my back against the wall, before violently slamming back into the rain-soaked plaza.

Cold sweat drenches my collar.

"Barry?" Hadrik stops, looking back at me with a furrowed brow. "You look like you've seen a ghost."

"You didn't... feel that?" I ask, steadying my breath.

"Feel what?"

"Nothing," I mutter, wiping rain and cold sweat from my forehead. "Just catching my balance. Let's catch up."

Hadrik grunts and turns forward.

A fluke. A residual neurological echo. Hadrik felt nothing, which means the Overseer isn't running another displacement wave. Even if the flash was real, I was anchored safely inside the cube. The ether can't reach inside.

I lengthen my stride to fall in behind Daren. Over the next hour, as we march through the dark streets toward the royal palace, the perspective shift does not return.

A harmless echo. Nothing more.

Everything is fine.

